      "id": 1,
      "text": "Oshawa began in 1876 as the McLaughlin Carriage Company, and today General Motors of Canada’s headquarters sit in the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshawa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Parkwood Estate National Historic Site, where GM founder R. J. McLaughlin was born, is a must‑visit for history buffs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshawa",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1937, 4,000 Oshawa workers staged a strike against General Motors that became a landmark moment in Canadian labor history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshawa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Filmmakers have used Oshawa’s streets and studios for hits like X‑Men, Chicago, and the Stephen King‑based film It.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oshawa",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Canadian Automotive Museum and the Ontario Regiment Museum showcase Oshawa’s rich industrial and military heritage on Lake Ontario’s shores.",
